Every neighbourhood of the universal system is the disjoint union of two neighbourhoods, each a nonempty proper subset of it.

Take a point of the neighbourhood and an interval of a presenting list that contains it, and split at the midpoint of that interval. The part of the presented set strictly below the midpoint and the part from the midpoint upward are disjoint, because a rational cannot lie both strictly below and at least at the midpoint, and they cover the set by comparing an arbitrary point with the midpoint. The left endpoint of the chosen interval lies in the lower part, and the midpoint lies in the upper part. Each part is a neighbourhood because the corresponding clipping identity presents it and the original inclusion in the unit interval restricts. Neither part equals the whole set, since equality would force the other part into the empty intersection.
